e we’re drawing, opponent unlikely to bluff if we check.  \n- *Bet min 100*: Small bet might get folds from weak hands like 7x, 6x, maybe even Q weak kicker if they’re passive, but more likely they call with any piece. We gain 100 into 600, risk 100 to maybe win 600 if they fold. Fold equity low vs calling station, but if they fold 30% of time, +EV.  \n- *All-in*: Massive overbet (19,600 into 600) would only be called by hands beating us (sets, two pair, maybe Q with good kicker). No fold equity because it’s absurd sizing, and we have outs if called. So going all-in turns a draw into a gamble with terrible pot odds offered to opponent but terrible for us if called.  \n\n**Reasoning:**  \nWe’re behind most of opponent’s continuing range from flop call. Betting small can sometimes take it down now, but our hand has showdown value if we hit straight, and we’re in position (check lets us realize equity). Checking is standard with a draw and no need to protect. Given opponent’s passivity, checking seems best to realize equity cheaply.  \n\n---\n\nEvaluation: Checking is the best choice to see a free river and preserve chips.  \n\nReason: We have a gutshot straight draw with some overcard outs, no need to bloat the pot when behind, and opponent's checking suggests weakness but not likely to fold to a small bet.  \n\nAnswer: 0"}
